  "notes": "The producer who took future house from a Beatport tag to the pop charts. The tempo is the tightest in the producer block because the entry samples his catalogue directly — readings of 126 / 126 / 125 / 128 / 125 / 124, \"124-128, centred on 125-126\" — so the mode is 126. The genre-defining parameter is authored where the entry puts it, in the bass: \"a metallic, elastic-sounding frequency-modulated bassline — a mono saw/square with heavy filter-envelope and pitch/FM movement per note, sidechained to the kick, playing an offbeat-8th or syncopated 16th figure. The bass *is* the lead.\" That is a 0.45 root probability with a 0.45 glide — the highest glide on any four-on-the-floor model here, one glide per note being exactly what \"pitch/FM movement per note\" means — and it is why \"the drop hook is the bass, not a supersaw\", which is the low melody density above it. The kit follows from the same logic: \"percussion is deliberately sparse so the bass hook reads as the hook\", built \"from one-shot samples on an MPC-style grid rather than loops\", with clap or snap on 2&4, 16th closed hats, the offbeat open hat and a shaker/tambourine 16th layer under the drop. Loops are 8 bars, and the arrangement is a \"bass-drop rather than a lead-drop\". ⚠ The research says the drum and bass grammar is future-house convention plus a teardown of the idiom; that mark is repeated here, and the velocities, densities and registers are INFERRED.",
